Carcinogenic Health Risks
The risk of cancer is the result of an average
lifetime exposure to 1 mg/kg body
weight/day of a pollutant. Incremental
lifetime cancer risk (ILCR), was used to
quantify cancer risk in this studies. ILCR
below 1x10-6 are deemed inconsequential
and can be ignored, but those above 1x10-4
are deemed harmful, according to USEPA
(1999), Eze et al. (2021), and Nawaz et al.
(2023).

Conversely, low risk of Pb was discovered
in Borehole, Vendor, and Rainwater, while
acceptable risk was found in the majority of
sources for Pb in infants’ age group.
However, while lead in tap, well, and sachet
water is within an acceptable limit for
infants, it poses a harmful risk to adults and
children. This study's findings about the
harmful hazards of Pb and Cd in certain
water sources are in line with a study
conducted in Nigeria by Etchie et al. (2018),
which identified these metals as significant
causes of cancer risk in urban water supplies.

